~ start
DODONG: Are you done buying yet?
- Yeah, all done!
	OLLIE: Yeah, I got everything already. Let's go!
	DODONG: Finally! You took so long my gameboy is almost dead.
	DODONG: So, did you spend all your money?
- Not yet, wait a bit.
	OLLIE: Not yet! Just wait a little bit more, I'm almost finished.

NONI: Yeah we ha-
NONI: Oh! Tatay is here!

Our old top-down farm vehicle pulls up in front of us. Tatay sits behind the wheel as he looks tired and serious after a long day at the coconut farm. 

When he leans closer, he smells like hot farm sweat and that strong tobacco he likes to smoke. He has dried mud caked all over his knuckles. It makes his skin look just like bumpy tree bark.

ERNESTO: Put your bags in the back. Hurry up, we need to beat the traffic before the main road gets bad.

OLLIE: Hello, Tay! Did you finish harvesting the coconuts today?

ERNESTO: Yes, but there is always more to do. The weather is always changing so we had to haul the copra fast before the rain ruins it. We cannot afford any damaged crops right now. 

Papa frowns as he looks down at what we brought from the stalls. He rests his forehead against the steering wheel for a quick second. He let out a sigh that sounded like it came from his bones. 

ERNESTO: I told you to save your extra coins. Did you really have to waste money on those street stalls again? 
ERNESTO: Money doesn't just appear out of nowhere, children. Every peso you spend out here is a coconut I had to climb and harvest under the blistering sun just so that we have food on the table. My hands are hard like rock just so your hands can stay soft. 

DODONG: No, Tay... I was just watching Noni near the stalls. They... they just wanted a little snack.

ERNESTO: The world doesn't care if we are tired or hungry. If the rain pours tonight and ruins our crops or if the middlemen lower the market price tomorrow, nobody will help us. The bank will not care and the vendors will not give us free food. 
ERNESTO: Games and toys will not buy our rice. Don't waste money on things we don't need anymore. 
ERNESTO: I am doing this for our family's sake. For your sake. 
ERNESTO: …

Oh...

I looked down at my soft fingers and quickly hid them in my pockets. I wished they were covered in mud too so Tatay wouldn't have to climb all those tall trees alone. 

Every coin I waste means Tatay has to climb another coconut tree until his hands look like that just to take care of us. 

Okay, maybe I can fix this. I'll be good.

OLLIE: I'm sorry, Tay. I was the one who insisted Kuya. I won't do it again.

Tatay's expression softens just a tiny bit before he turns back to the road and shifts the gear.

ERNESTO: Just climb up and hold onto the rails tightly. Let's go home, your mother is waiting.

I don't say anything back. 

The three of us scramble and climb up into the back of the vehicle together. Kuya and Noni sit on the benches but I squeeze myself right into the corner right next to the scratchy coconut sacks.

=> END